78
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,400
Well, in the remainder of this week's podcast,

79
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:33,000
I've got some email questions to answer,

80
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,800
So let's get started, shall we?

81
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:38,000
Across the nation, communities and the parents of Boy Scouts

82
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:39,900
are observing Boy Scout Week,

83
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:44,400
Agreeing with the boys themselves that adventure, that scouting.

84
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:23,400
Write me a letter, send it by name.

85
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:25,700
Email, that is folks.

86
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:29,200
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

87
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:41,200
This first email question comes from Jeff and he says:

88
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:44,500
as a scout master, I continually have this issue.

89
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:48,200
Our Scouts move through the ranks and that position of responsibility

90
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:51,000
is needed, typically a four or six month term.

91
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,700
Our leadership term runs October through March and April through September

92
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:59,000
After the senior patrol leader election.

93
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,800
I encourage the Scouts to write up a brief paragraph

94
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,000
of the leadership role they're interested in

95
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
and why the senior patrol leaders should appoint them.

96
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
I will sit down with the senior patrol leader

97
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,200
and review the submissions and any leadership positions available.

98
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:15,800
If a scout has not spoken to the senior patrol leader

99
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:17,400
or submitted a leadership request,

100
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:20,500
they are usually not chosen for a leadership position.

101
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:23,600
Some Scouts have even indicated that they do not want

102
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:25,800
a leadership role due to sports conflicts

103
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:28,100
or if they want to take a break from being a leader.

104
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:31,200
Now my issue is the performance of the youth leaders.

105
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:34,000
Several youth leaders don't attend patrol leaders council,

106
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:35,600
don't go on camp outs,

107
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:37,500
attend less than half of the troop meetings

108
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:39,800
or do nothing in their leadership role.

109
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:43,100
Now I speak with them and remind them of their responsibilities

110
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:45,500
as the leadership term progresses.

111
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:48,300
Later I will get a request for a scout master's conference

112
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:49,100
and I will ask them:

113
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:51,600
well, what have you done with your leadership role?

114
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:53,600
They usually will respond with nothing,

115
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:57,400
so I will not sign off and recommend them for a border review?

116
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:00,800
I have one scout's been a historian for the past year.

117
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:02,600
During the last two years

118
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:04,200
he's been on zero camp outs,

119
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:06,600
attended 15% of troop meetings

120
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:09,700
and has not produced anything in the historian role.

121
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:12,400
Now this is typically a scrapbook with photos and writeups

122
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:14,100
of what's been happening during the year.

123
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:16,000
I've spoken with him multiple times,

124
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,900
gently reminding him of his role and responsibilities.

125
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:20,600
He said that he'll do better

126
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:23,200
than he's absent for the next four to six weeks.

127
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:26,600
He's now asking for a scout master conference for life rank

128
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:29,000
and I've asked him to show me the efforts of his role

129
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,700
as a historian before I recommend him for a border review.

130
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:35,900
So this brings me to my two questions:

131
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:39,100
Am I being out of line for refusing to sign for a scout

132
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:42,200
who has not done anything in a leadership role,

133
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:46,600
and how do you encourage scouts to fulfill a leadership role?

134
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:50,100
Well, Jeff, let's start with the second question first.

135
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:53,300
I think the answer to the question-

136
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:56,300
how do you encourage scouts to fulfill leadership role-

137
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:59,800
is more about the perspective that you have

138
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:01,599
on the way these positions work.

139
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:03,699
When there's things to do

140
00:09:03,699 --> 00:09:06,099
that are vital, practical and engaging,

141
00:09:06,099 --> 00:09:08,199
you know scouts will roll up their sleeves

142
00:09:08,199 --> 00:09:10,000
and get things moving.

143
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,800
If the role that they're in isn't vital

144
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:16,800
or practical or engaging,

145
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,599
they don't pay too much attention to those roles,

146
00:09:19,599 --> 00:09:22,099
And you know who would.

147
00:09:22,099 --> 00:09:25,099
If it's not going to be something that is valuable

148
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:30,400
to me as a scout or to making things happen,

149
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:32,800
why am I going to be bothered with it?

150
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:35,100
That's just the way. I think at least

151
00:09:35,100 --> 00:09:38,100
Most of us find dealing with the issues

152
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:39,700
that you've outlined challenging,

153
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:42,400
So let's see if we can unwind some of this.

154
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:44,400
The first challenge is to understand.

155
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:47,100
there are no leadership positions mentioned

156
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:48,700
in any of the rank requirements,

157
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:50,800
Only positions of responsibility,

158
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:52,600
And that's an important distinction,

159
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:54,700
because not every one of the positions

160
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:57,900
of responsibility necessarily require

161
00:09:57,900 --> 00:10:02,400
what we would normally identify as leadership skills.

162
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:04,500
The ones that do, it's pretty obvious

163
00:10:04,500 --> 00:10:05,900
because they have the word leader in them,

164
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:07,600
like patrol leader, senior patrol leader,

165
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:09,000
assistant senior patrol leader.

166
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,300
That's not to say that guys occupying other roles

167
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:14,200
won't have leadership qualities and exercise them,

168
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:16,000
But I think it's an important distinction.

169
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,500
So let's talk about roles of responsibility

170
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,300
And when we talk about these,

171
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:24,200
the first question ought to be what needs to be done,

172
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:26,800
not who needs to fulfill a rank requirement

173
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:29,000
or who submitted a leadership request,

174
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,500
And we addressed this a couple of podcasts back.

175
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:35,300
You know, youth leaders aren't employees of the troop,

176
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:36,900
They're active players in a game.

177
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:40,300
I get the idea of submitting a leadership request

178
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:42,500
on a piece of paper, but really

179
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:45,200
to me it invites all kinds of associations

180
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:50,500
with business and industry and employees.

181
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:54,500
I would stay as far away from that as possible.

182
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,500
Now, if you remember you go back a couple of podcasts.

183
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:59,500
We talked about this responsibility as the horse.

184
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:00,900
The program is the cart

185
00:11:00,900 --> 00:11:03,500
And advancement is the track. the cart leaves behind.

186
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:06,300
A horse without a cart or a cart without a horse

187
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:08,700
is not going to leave the right kind of tracks right.

188
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:10,100
So we need the combination,

189
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:12,900
We need them all put into perspective,

190
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:14,900
We need them hooked up the right way.

191
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:17,100
But there has to be something to do.

192
00:11:17,100 --> 00:11:19,300
There has to be a program to do it for

193
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,100
And then you get the results that you're looking for.

194
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:23,500
The program's there,

195
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:26,300
It's ready to go, but it can't go on its own.

196
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:29,900
It's all words and books and words on pieces of paper.

197
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:33,300
It doesn't have muscles or lungs or a heart.

198
00:11:33,300 --> 00:11:35,500
The program is just sitting there.

199
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:38,600
Responsibility is where all the heart and muscle is.

200
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:40,500
That's the way things happen.

201
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,400
Advancement can't go on its own.

202
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:43,700
It's the result of action,

203
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:45,900
It's not the actions themselves.

204
00:11:45,900 --> 00:11:49,200
The positions of responsibility are there to make things go,

205
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:51,800
Not to parse out advancement opportunities.

206
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:56,200
So encouraging scouts to do something about their position of responsibility

207
00:11:56,200 --> 00:11:59,600
has to do with the nature of the position itself and your perspective.

208
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:03,000
It can't just be why don't you do your job,

209
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,100
You know. it can't just be that.

210
00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:12,300
It has to be something that a scout sees as being vital and engaging

211
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:15,800
and important to the life of his patrol and his troop.

212
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:19,100
The first question you asked- which I am answering second

213
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:20,900
is everybody keeping notes- was:

214
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:24,300
am I being out of line for refusing to sign for a scout

215
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:27,200
who's not done anything in a leadership role?

216
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:31,000
When a scout hasn't achieved our expectations,

217
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:36,400
we have to first ask if those expectations were actively shared

218
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:39,200
or just kind of passively implied.

219
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:42,800
I mean, you can't hand a scout a sheet of paper with expectations.

220
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:44,000
That never works.

221
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,200
You have to actively discuss those expectations

222
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:53,000
and then get them to develop a set of shared expectations.

223
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,900
You have to collaborate on this.

224
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:58,800
It's our responsibility to assure that from the get-go

225
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:02,900
there's a shared expectation that the scouts understood them

226
00:13:02,900 --> 00:13:06,200
and then, to you know, monitor their progress

227
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:08,500
in achieving those expectations.

228
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:12,300
So in the particular instance that you talk about, Jeff.

229
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:15,300
you've got a scout who has gone for a year without doing anything

230
00:13:15,300 --> 00:13:19,600
and I've got to say that's actually more our fault as scouters

231
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:20,900
than anything else.

232
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:23,600
Allowing a scout to remain in the position for a year

233
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:27,000
without requiring any results is a very clear message to him.

234
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:28,700
There's no problem with the way you're doing this.

235
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:32,000
I guarantee that is what this scout thinks,

236
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,000
because he's asking you for a scout master's conference

237
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:36,900
for life rank.

238
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:38,700
So he's pretty sure there's no problem.

239
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:41,300
Now you say that you gave him some reminders

240
00:13:41,300 --> 00:13:42,300
and you discussed with him,

241
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:44,600
You encouraged him and everything, and that's great.

242
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:48,600
But scout age boys are not susceptible to gentle reminders

243
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:49,600
for the most part.

244
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:53,000
Most of the time they have to have things very clearly

245
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:53,700
spelled out.

246
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:55,400
They are literalists,

247
00:13:55,400 --> 00:13:57,200
They do not take hints.

248
00:13:57,200 --> 00:14:00,600
There has to be a continual engagement and evaluation,

249
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:04,800
because scouts do not have a good handle on their schedules.

250
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:07,400
Their level of excitement about responsibilities

251
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:11,200
is highly variable and you can be very direct about this

252
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:12,500
without being unpleasant.

253
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:15,900
One of my stock questions is when I'm concerned

254
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:19,100
that a position of responsibility is not really

255
00:14:19,100 --> 00:14:22,600
being actively taken on is to say, hey, what have you done

256
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:25,600
towards fulfilling your responsibilities this past week?

257
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:28,100
A lot of times the answer is: well, I've been really busy,

258
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:29,500
I've got a lot of homework,

259
00:14:29,500 --> 00:14:32,900
I'm playing football, whatever,

260
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:35,500
And I just haven't had time to do anything.

261
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:36,700
And you know what?

262
00:14:36,700 --> 00:14:37,700
That's fine.

263
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:40,800
And I say that. I say, look, we all have weeks like that.

264
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:41,900
Let's look at next week.

265
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:44,699
What specifically will you be doing this week

266
00:14:44,699 --> 00:14:45,900
And when will you do it?

267
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:49,500
What will I be able to ask you the next time we get together-

268
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:52,300
And in the instance that you shared, Jeff, you're talking

269
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:54,500
about a historian who's supposed to be making a scrapbook.

270
00:14:54,500 --> 00:14:57,199
Maybe he tells you: you know what?

271
00:14:57,199 --> 00:15:00,300
I'm going to get a scrapbook on Wednesday and get started.

272
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:01,199
That's great.

273
00:15:01,199 --> 00:15:02,800
Bring the scrapbook with you next week.

274
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:04,900
Show me how much progress you've made

275
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:07,300
Now. if he hasn't made any progress by the next week-

276
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:09,900
and this is probably exactly what will happen-

277
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:12,199
If he's not interested or engaged in the whole thing

278
00:15:12,199 --> 00:15:13,100
next week, he'll show up.

279
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:14,699
You'll say: did you bring the scrapbook?

280
00:15:14,699 --> 00:15:16,900
No, didn't have time to get it.

281
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:18,100
Then I would tell him this:

282
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:20,500
Look, if you don't have the time to do this right now

283
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:23,699
or you're not really interested in doing this, I understand.

284
00:15:23,699 --> 00:15:25,900
There's no problem. no harm, no foul.

285
00:15:25,900 --> 00:15:28,199
Would you like to pass this on to someone else,

286
00:15:28,199 --> 00:15:30,900
Or do you think you really want us to be able to continue?

287
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:33,500
If the answer to that question is the affirmative yes,

288
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:35,199
I really want to continue in this-

289
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:40,900
Then the deal is: something must happen by next week.

290
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:43,100
That's a non-negotiable term.

291
00:15:43,100 --> 00:15:45,100
Something must happen.

292
00:15:45,100 --> 00:15:48,300
You need to show me what's happened next week.

293
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:51,500
If the answer is no, then that's fine.

294
00:15:51,500 --> 00:15:53,300
Tell them you'll work with the senior patrol leader

295
00:15:53,300 --> 00:15:55,900
past the responsibility onto somebody else

296
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:57,800
and when this guy is ready,

297
00:15:57,800 --> 00:16:00,100
when he has the time and the interest,

298
00:16:00,100 --> 00:16:01,900
we'll get them a responsibility.

299
00:16:01,900 --> 00:16:04,000
Now, if week three rolls around

300
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:05,800
and there's no progress,

301
00:16:05,800 --> 00:16:08,500
I'm going to say that we've discussed how important this is

302
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:12,200
for you to make some progress on getting this done.

303
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:14,600
If you can't honestly do it, I understand

304
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:17,000
and apparently that's the way that it is,

305
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,400
But there is no way we go on past next week.

306
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:23,600
Specifically, what can I expect?

307
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:25,400
When will we talk next week

308
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:27,200
And then get an answer,

309
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:31,300
have a shared expectation about what's going to happen

310
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:33,900
and say, look, that's great.

311
00:16:33,900 --> 00:16:36,400
I want to make sure you understand something:

312
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:39,699
If we get to next week with zero progress,

313
00:16:39,699 --> 00:16:42,000
then I'm going to tell the senior patrol leader

314
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,500
to pass this responsibility onto someone else.

315
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:46,800
Have you heard and understand exactly

316
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:48,400
what our agreement is here.

317
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:50,800
So let me go back, because this is kind of a step one,

318
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:52,000
two, three, four thing,

319
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,400
which I'm not normally very fond of.

320
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:57,000
but listen to the spirit behind the thing.

321
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,300
The first time out is to say:

322
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:00,500
so, what have you done?

323
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:03,100
If the answer is I haven't done anything,

324
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:04,400
then you say: okay,

325
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:06,500
so what's your plan for next week

326
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:08,099
and what will you have done?

327
00:17:08,099 --> 00:17:11,200
And you have a collaboration,

328
00:17:11,200 --> 00:17:13,400
you create a shared expectation.

329
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:15,000
The next week rolls around,

330
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:16,700
likely, it's not, nothing's happened.

331
00:17:16,700 --> 00:17:19,000
You say, okay, yeah, I know

332
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
We all have weeks like this.

333
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,400
Let's get back up and running next week.

334
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:26,599
Definitely this is going to happen.

335
00:17:26,599 --> 00:17:28,400
Then we get to that next week,

336
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:30,000
nothing's happened. again.

337
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:31,900
And then you say, okay,

338
00:17:31,900 --> 00:17:35,500
look, this is what must happen by next week.

339
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:36,900
If it doesn't happen,

340
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:38,800
I'm instructing the senior patrol leader

341
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:40,900
to pass the responsibility on to someone else,

342
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:43,200
because you're either not interested

343
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:45,200
or you don't have the time, and that's fine,

344
00:17:45,200 --> 00:17:47,100
And there'll be a position of responsibility

345
00:17:47,100 --> 00:17:48,600
for you when you do have the time,

346
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:50,000
and one that you're interested in.

347
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,600
If you don't have any results for me next week,

348
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:54,100
that's what's going to happen

349
00:17:54,100 --> 00:17:55,300
Now. do you understand that?

350
00:17:55,300 --> 00:17:57,600
Do we have a shared expectation.

351
00:17:57,600 --> 00:17:59,600
Now, if that scout never shows up,

352
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:00,800
I've got to pick up the phone

353
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:02,399
and have that conversation right.

354
00:18:02,399 --> 00:18:05,000
But there has to be a point of action

355
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,500
or a point of moving on

356
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:10,700
And, Jeff, I would suggest consider shortening the time

357
00:18:10,700 --> 00:18:13,700
that scouts spend in particular positions

358
00:18:13,700 --> 00:18:15,100
of responsibility.

359
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:17,300
Six months or a year is really an eternity

360
00:18:17,300 --> 00:18:18,899
for a scout-age boy,

361
00:18:18,899 --> 00:18:22,100
And even most adults that I know now

362
00:18:22,100 --> 00:18:24,500
would find making an absolute commitment

363
00:18:24,500 --> 00:18:27,000
for the next year or the next six months

364
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,600
to be very challenging,

365
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:29,600
if not impossible,

366
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:31,100
because there's a number of commitments

367
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:33,400
a scout or an adult has to balance

368
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:36,000
in taking on any kind of responsibility

369
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,700
and making a commitment towards it.

370
00:18:37,700 --> 00:18:40,200
You can think that that's a horrible character flaw.

371
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:41,900
The way that our lives are ordered

372
00:18:41,900 --> 00:18:44,500
in this day and age is very complex.

373
00:18:44,500 --> 00:18:47,300
The way a family's life works is very complex.

374
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:50,500
The way a young person's life works is very complex,

375
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:52,600
And if you want to fight with that complexity,

376
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:53,800
go ahead.

377
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:55,200
I don't want to fight with it.

378
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:57,500
I want to make scouting work

379
00:18:57,500 --> 00:18:59,400
for every single young person

380
00:18:59,400 --> 00:19:00,800
that I can make it work for,

381
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:01,600
And I can do that

382
00:19:01,600 --> 00:19:04,400
without diminishing expectations or standards.

383
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:06,900
It's not that difficult to do.

384
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:08,500
I'll tell you what we've been doing

385
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:09,500
and experimenting with.

386
00:19:09,500 --> 00:19:11,700
We fill positions of responsibility

387
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:13,000
every three months

388
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:13,800
And we do that

389
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:15,900
based on the commitments

390
00:19:15,900 --> 00:19:17,400
for going camping

391
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:18,900
for the next three months

392
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:19,400
And that way

393
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:20,300
there's no question

394
00:19:20,300 --> 00:19:22,500
as to whether or not key youth leaders

395
00:19:22,500 --> 00:19:24,600
will be on camping trips

396
00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:25,400
or at meetings,

397
00:19:25,400 --> 00:19:27,000
because they can't accept

398
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
a position of responsibility

399
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,800
unless they're committed to being on all

400
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:32,700
the camping trips

401
00:19:32,700 --> 00:19:34,200
and at all the meetings,

402
00:19:34,200 --> 00:19:35,400
or at least you know

403
00:19:35,400 --> 00:19:36,800
the majority of the meetings.

404
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:38,900
And that's for a three month period of time

405
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:40,600
And it roughly corresponds

406
00:19:40,600 --> 00:19:42,000
to the quarters at school,

407
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:43,600
So that school activities,

408
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:46,200
extracurricular activities at school,

409
00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:47,500
kind of mesh with that.

410
00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:48,600
So you know

411
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:50,400
if you're going to be in the marching band,

412
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:51,400
you're going to play football,

413
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:52,600
you're going to be a wrestler,

414
00:19:52,600 --> 00:19:54,300
you're going to be a basketball player

415
00:19:54,300 --> 00:19:56,600
and that's going to consume all of your free time

416
00:19:56,600 --> 00:19:57,399
outside of school

417
00:19:57,399 --> 00:19:58,500
for the next three months,

418
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:00,200
which is usually what the season is.

419
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:01,200
No harm, no foul.

420
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:02,100
We expect that.

421
00:20:02,100 --> 00:20:03,000
But you know,

422
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:04,399
to make things work smoothly

423
00:20:04,399 --> 00:20:05,399
for the patrols

424
00:20:05,399 --> 00:20:06,399
and for the troop,

425
00:20:06,399 --> 00:20:08,700
we're asking for a commitment upfront.

426
00:20:08,700 --> 00:20:09,600
Tell us what you're doing

427
00:20:09,600 --> 00:20:10,500
for the next three months

428
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:11,800
and then we'll make some decisions

429
00:20:11,800 --> 00:20:13,700
about who it would be appropriate

430
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:16,100
to offer positions of responsibility.

431
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:17,899
I mean when we work with scouts,

432
00:20:17,899 --> 00:20:19,200
we have to accept the idea

433
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:20,300
that they likely have

434
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:22,600
no earthly idea

435
00:20:22,600 --> 00:20:24,800
of the things that we take for granted.

436
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:27,900
A few scouts are really capable and responsible,

437
00:20:27,900 --> 00:20:30,000
but most need a lot of experience

438
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:31,700
before they truly understand

439
00:20:31,700 --> 00:20:33,400
the dynamics of all this.

440
00:20:33,400 --> 00:20:35,900
because we are familiar with business practices,

441
00:20:35,900 --> 00:20:36,700
We tend to use them

442
00:20:36,700 --> 00:20:37,600
when dealing with scouts

443
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:39,100
and positions of responsibility,

444
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:41,100
but I'm going to say it again

445
00:20:41,100 --> 00:20:42,300
and I'll keep saying it,

446
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:43,600
and over and over:

447
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:45,000
They are not our employees.

448
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:46,200
Scouts is not a business.

449
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:47,800
You are not the boss.

450
00:20:47,800 --> 00:20:50,200
Positions of responsibility- and scouting,

451
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:51,200
to me at least,

452
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:52,300
is about working together

453
00:20:52,300 --> 00:20:53,800
to get things done.

454
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:55,200
They're not about title

455
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:56,100
or privilege

456
00:20:56,100 --> 00:20:57,800
or command structures.

457
00:20:57,800 --> 00:21:00,400
It's all just this big grand game

458
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:01,600
that we get to be the coach

459
00:21:01,600 --> 00:21:02,700
and the mentor.

460
00:21:02,700 --> 00:21:04,200
We collaborate with scouts

461
00:21:04,200 --> 00:21:06,300
to realize the wonderful opportunities

462
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:07,700
the scouting offers.

463
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:08,600
and this happens

464
00:21:08,600 --> 00:21:09,800
one scout at a time,

465
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:11,500
one conversation at a time,

466
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:13,100
one week at a time,

467
00:21:13,100 --> 00:21:16,000
and when we focus our efforts

468
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
on making scouts successful

469
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,500
in these positions of responsibility

470
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:22,400
rather than laying down a gauntlet

471
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:23,400
and challenging them

472
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:24,400
to be successful.

473
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:27,400
No, we focus on making them successful,

474
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:28,200
helping them out

475
00:21:28,200 --> 00:21:29,800
as much as possible

476
00:21:29,800 --> 00:21:32,900
and making our expectations realistic,

477
00:21:32,900 --> 00:21:34,700
considering the complexity

478
00:21:34,700 --> 00:21:35,400
of their lives

479
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:36,900
and their family's lives.

480
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,200
It's not an easy subject, is it?

481
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:42,300
It's not easy to do

482
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:43,800
and it shouldn't be easy to do.

483
00:21:43,800 --> 00:21:45,200
It's one of the bigger challenges

484
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:46,400
of being a scouter.

485
00:21:46,400 --> 00:21:47,600
The next question

486
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:49,000
comes from

487
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Brian Frey,

488
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
who is the committee chair of crew 808

489
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:53,800
in Mililani, Hawaii,

490
00:21:53,800 --> 00:21:54,600
and he says:

491
00:21:54,600 --> 00:21:56,400
I'm working with some energetic youth

492
00:21:56,400 --> 00:21:57,500
and dedicated scouts

493
00:21:57,500 --> 00:21:58,700
to get a new venturing crew

494
00:21:58,700 --> 00:22:00,700
established here in Hawaii

495
00:22:00,700 --> 00:22:02,400
and I've accepted the position

496
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:03,600
of crew committee chair.

497
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:04,800
The crew president-

498
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:05,700
and if you're not familiar

499
00:22:05,700 --> 00:22:07,400
with the way venture crews work, folks,

500
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:09,700
the crew president is a youth leader.

501
00:22:09,700 --> 00:22:11,000
The crew president

502
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:12,200
would like to move away

503
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:14,200
from what our troop did,

504
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:16,100
which was to collect a permission slip

505
00:22:16,100 --> 00:22:18,100
for each scouting event.

506
00:22:18,100 --> 00:22:19,800
This proved to be time consuming

507
00:22:19,800 --> 00:22:20,800
and kind of frustrating

508
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:22,300
for scouts and adult leaders

509
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:23,500
organizing the event.

510
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:24,900
The new procedure

511
00:22:24,900 --> 00:22:26,600
would be to have the parent or guardian

512
00:22:26,600 --> 00:22:28,200
sign one permission slip

513
00:22:28,200 --> 00:22:30,100
for their youth annually.

514
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:31,500
This one time consent

515
00:22:31,500 --> 00:22:32,700
would cover all the events

516
00:22:32,700 --> 00:22:33,600
for the year.

517
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:34,500
I'm a little concerned

518
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:36,300
about this kind of approach.

519
00:22:36,300 --> 00:22:38,100
I see the BSA

520
00:22:38,100 --> 00:22:40,500
has an activity consent form

521
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:42,900
that looks like a per event form,

522
00:22:42,900 --> 00:22:44,900
but it doesn't state that explicitly.

523
00:22:44,900 --> 00:22:46,400
I'm curious to learn

524
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:47,700
if you've had any experience

525
00:22:47,700 --> 00:22:48,600
with this type of thing

526
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:49,800
and, if so,

527
00:22:49,800 --> 00:22:51,000
what is your approach?

528
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:51,600
Well, Brian,

529
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:52,300
I don't know

530
00:22:52,300 --> 00:22:54,100
that there's any specific policy

531
00:22:54,100 --> 00:22:55,600
that would prevent anyone

532
00:22:55,600 --> 00:22:58,300
from doing an annual permission slip,

533
00:22:58,300 --> 00:23:00,200
but I don't really think

534
00:23:00,200 --> 00:23:01,600
it's very practical.

535
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:02,500
Anybody who's

536
00:23:02,500 --> 00:23:03,700
listened to the podcast

537
00:23:03,700 --> 00:23:04,400
or read the blog

538
00:23:04,400 --> 00:23:05,800
knows I am a continual,

539
00:23:05,800 --> 00:23:07,500
dedicated foe

540
00:23:07,500 --> 00:23:09,300
of unnecessary paperwork-

541
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:10,900
and all paperwork in general,

542
00:23:10,900 --> 00:23:11,900
by the way-

543
00:23:11,900 --> 00:23:13,100
But I do understand

544
00:23:13,100 --> 00:23:15,000
that some paperwork is important.

545
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:16,300
Permission slips indicate

546
00:23:16,300 --> 00:23:18,000
to a third party

547
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,600
that a child is knowingly placed

548
00:23:20,600 --> 00:23:21,700
in the care of an adult

549
00:23:21,700 --> 00:23:22,600
other than a parent.

550
00:23:22,600 --> 00:23:24,000
That's important.

551
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:25,399
They also share information

552
00:23:25,399 --> 00:23:26,100
with families

553
00:23:26,100 --> 00:23:27,300
about where to drop off

554
00:23:27,300 --> 00:23:27,899
and pick up,

555
00:23:27,899 --> 00:23:29,600
how to contact their child,

556
00:23:29,600 --> 00:23:30,600
who's in charge

557
00:23:30,600 --> 00:23:32,100
and what they will be doing

558
00:23:32,100 --> 00:23:33,500
and where they will be.

559
00:23:33,500 --> 00:23:34,800
So I think

560
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:35,899
all of that information

561
00:23:35,899 --> 00:23:36,399
is important,

562
00:23:36,399 --> 00:23:37,700
but it doesn't necessarily

563
00:23:37,700 --> 00:23:38,800
only have to be shared

564
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:40,000
in a permission slip.

565
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:41,200
There's also the factor

566
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:42,899
of actually having

567
00:23:42,899 --> 00:23:44,600
absolute solid plans

568
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:46,100
a year in advance.

569
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:47,600
I've never been able to achieve that

570
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:49,900
because we've always had to change something

571
00:23:49,900 --> 00:23:52,300
and that may not be possible.

572
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:53,800
I know it would be for me

573
00:23:53,800 --> 00:23:56,100
and probably would be for our troop,

574
00:23:56,100 --> 00:23:57,600
What I mentioned in the previous answer-

575
00:23:57,600 --> 00:23:59,600
the email- bears on this a little bit,

576
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:01,100
because what we started doing

577
00:24:01,100 --> 00:24:04,300
is having a three month permission slip.

578
00:24:04,300 --> 00:24:05,600
So we have

579
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:07,000
three months of activities

580
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,100
outlined on the permission slip

581
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:10,500
with all of the information

582
00:24:10,500 --> 00:24:11,400
that I just mentioned:

583
00:24:11,400 --> 00:24:13,700
the who, why, what, where and how.

584
00:24:13,700 --> 00:24:14,800
It's a little bit lighter

585
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:16,300
lifting for families

586
00:24:16,300 --> 00:24:18,300
and it's easier to administrate.

587
00:24:18,300 --> 00:24:20,000
from our point of view,

588
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
Three months seems to be the limit

589
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:23,100
of a family's ability

590
00:24:23,100 --> 00:24:25,100
to look forward and plan

591
00:24:25,100 --> 00:24:26,600
and it's really challenging

592
00:24:26,600 --> 00:24:27,800
for some families.

593
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:28,700
There's a lot of things

594
00:24:28,700 --> 00:24:30,000
that recommend this idea

595
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:31,399
of looking three months ahead,

596
00:24:31,399 --> 00:24:33,500
getting commitments three months ahead,

597
00:24:33,500 --> 00:24:34,500
getting permission slips,

598
00:24:34,500 --> 00:24:35,700
taking care of getting payments

599
00:24:35,700 --> 00:24:36,899
three months ahead.

600
00:24:36,899 --> 00:24:38,700
It cuts down on a lot

601
00:24:38,700 --> 00:24:40,500
of fuss and bother

602
00:24:40,500 --> 00:24:42,700
of doing it every single month.

603
00:24:42,700 --> 00:24:44,100
It gives us

604
00:24:44,100 --> 00:24:46,100
a very concrete idea

605
00:24:46,100 --> 00:24:48,000
of what our participation will be

606
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:49,200
for a given activity

607
00:24:49,200 --> 00:24:51,000
and, as I said in the answer

608
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:52,100
to the previous email,

609
00:24:52,100 --> 00:24:54,400
helps us work with our scouts

610
00:24:54,400 --> 00:24:56,700
to set up positions of responsibility

611
00:24:56,700 --> 00:24:57,600
that are achievable

612
00:24:57,600 --> 00:24:59,000
in a three month period.

613
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:00,500
and we are beginning

614
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:02,600
our second quarter of doing this.

615
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:03,300
the first quarter

616
00:25:03,300 --> 00:25:04,600
it worked out pretty well.

617
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:05,400
The second quarter

618
00:25:05,400 --> 00:25:06,600
took a little bit longer

619
00:25:06,600 --> 00:25:08,000
to get off the ground with it

620
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:09,300
because we were also dealing

621
